00:00:26.035 --> 00:00:28.160
AMIT GANDHI: When you have
a group of two or three,

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.970
come grab a soldering iron
and your solar cells from me

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:36.800
and choose, like, somewhere
in this room to work.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.030
We're staying here for this.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.385
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:00:40.385 --> 00:00:42.010
AUDIENCE: Do you want
us to overlay it?

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:43.207
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:00:43.207 --> 00:00:45.540
AMIT GANDHI: Oh, you're not
actually soldering directly.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:46.748
You don't want to overlay it.

00:00:46.748 --> 00:00:47.870
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:50.120
AUDIENCE: So these three
contacts will be in parallel,

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.160
and these will be
in series, right?

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.530
AMIT GANDHI: Yes, you
generally want to go, like--

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:56.787
the way you would generally
do it is you would--

00:00:56.787 --> 00:00:58.370
so I think it's less
critical that you

00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:59.550
cover this entire side.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:00.527
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:02.360
AMIT GANDHI: So you
cover a little bit of it

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.050
and solder to that.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:08.090
And the back is also, like,
all this is conductive.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:09.590
The only reason
the tabs are there

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:11.990
are because the tabs
are easier to solder to.

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:14.600
And so it's just
more convenient.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.718
And you don't really need
to solder to all of them

00:01:17.718 --> 00:01:18.260
individually.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:23.900
You can only-- like, at minimal,
if you wanted to do it really

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.950
minimally, you could
just do a solder from--

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:29.340
a wire from there to there.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:30.800
And that would work.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:31.940
Like, that would be fine.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:32.517
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:01:32.517 --> 00:01:34.850
AMIT GANDHI: But the reason
that they have a lot of them

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:37.490
is because if something cracks
or something like that, then

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:39.532
you can tell-- if you have
a wire running across,

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:43.160
your wire is not going to crack
because these are fragile.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.463
And so you have more--

00:01:44.463 --> 00:01:45.630
you also have more contacts.

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:46.160
So--

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:47.210
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:50.720
AMIT GANDHI: So you should
feel free to wire it however

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:51.872
you want.

00:01:51.872 --> 00:01:55.480
And we're going to
test them later too.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:56.480
AUDIENCE: Is it melting?

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.550
AUDIENCE: Grab that [INAUDIBLE]
so you could dial it up.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:00.050
AUDIENCE: Yeah we
dialed this up.

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:02.750
AUDIENCE: You want to-- can we?

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:04.190
AUDIENCE: Across
these two, right?

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.250
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:08.750
AUDIENCE: Or do you want us to
cut it off into small strips?

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:11.190
AMIT GANDHI: I'm trying to
think if that would work.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.740
AUDIENCE: Then
connect these two.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:13.910
AMIT GANDHI: But I don't--
like, I don't know if that would

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:14.725
work.

00:02:14.725 --> 00:02:16.100
AUDIENCE: It'd be
a full circuit.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:17.808
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah,
but I don't if you can

00:02:17.808 --> 00:02:19.370
do that across a specific cell.

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:20.960
Like, if you like--

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:23.210
that wouldn't work
because I mean,

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:27.890
it would definitely make
sense if you took two of them

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:31.430
and you did one one way
and you did the reverse.

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:34.850
You had them going in two
different patterns on one side.

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:36.685
So you were connecting
positive to negative

00:02:36.685 --> 00:02:37.685
to positive to negative.

00:02:37.685 --> 00:02:38.570
AUDIENCE: Uh-huh.

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:39.650
AMIT GANDHI: And then
on the other one,

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:41.317
you were connecting
negative to positive

00:02:41.317 --> 00:02:44.100
to negative to positive.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:44.910
AUDIENCE: Uh-huh.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:45.530
AUDIENCE: Lattice on top.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:46.740
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I don't know.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:47.860
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:51.294
AUDIENCE: Lattice on top.

00:02:51.294 --> 00:02:54.210
This on top.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:55.670
AUDIENCE: Oh.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:57.722
AUDIENCE: It's a very
[INAUDIBLE] on top.

00:02:57.722 --> 00:02:58.400
Like that.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.043
AUDIENCE: Do it
on here and here?

00:03:00.043 --> 00:03:00.710
AUDIENCE: Right?

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:02.270
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah.

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:03.340
AUDIENCE: Yeah, do we--

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:05.590
AMIT GANDHI: That's how you
would traditionally do it.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:09.350
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:10.870
AUDIENCE: Oh no, it was good.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:12.540
It was good until [INAUDIBLE].

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:13.790
It was good until [INAUDIBLE].

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.254
AUDIENCE: Can you
use the scissors?

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:27.540
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:03:34.163 --> 00:03:35.830
AUDIENCE: That's what
I'm talking about.

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:37.330
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I hate that.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:38.530
AUDIENCE: And then it
tries to burn the button.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:39.030
Yeah.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:41.802
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:51.158
AUDIENCE: Do we have
to do this side too?

00:03:51.158 --> 00:03:52.450
AUDIENCE: You need more solder?

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:52.750
AUDIENCE: Ribbon.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:53.583
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:03:53.583 --> 00:03:55.027
AUDIENCE: A little more ribbon.

00:03:55.027 --> 00:03:56.860
AMIT GANDHI: So you
should also probably cut

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:00.910
this shorter because this
is electrically conductive.

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:03.583
Even though it's
not tabbed, like,

00:04:03.583 --> 00:04:05.500
the entire surface is
electrically conductive.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.030
So if it's touching,
it's going to short.

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:08.500
AUDIENCE: So like,
this part, like, you

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:09.430
only need this one to touch?

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:10.623
Is what what you're saying?

00:04:10.623 --> 00:04:11.290
AMIT GANDHI: No.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:13.660
Well, that is true.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:16.438
So the thing is
that all of these

00:04:16.438 --> 00:04:18.730
are-- like, this is all one
surface that's electrically

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:19.269
conductive.

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:20.977
The tabs are just
there to make it easier

00:04:20.977 --> 00:04:24.190
to solder onto or supposedly
make it easier to solder onto.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:25.450
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:27.950
AMIT GANDHI: But the thing is
that if you have two of these,

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:32.487
like, right next to each other
and they're like the backs--

00:04:32.487 --> 00:04:34.570
like, there's a wire
touching-- like, this wire is

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:35.650
touching this.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:37.080
Then you're going
to short your--

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:37.780
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:39.220
AUDIENCE: So you should
just cut that part off.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:39.970
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:41.410
So here, you can do that part.

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:44.140
All you need to do is from here
to, like, this much length.

00:04:52.402 --> 00:04:56.625
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:04:56.625 --> 00:04:58.335
AUDIENCE: I heard
something crack.

00:04:58.335 --> 00:04:59.700
AUDIENCE: Uh-oh.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.540
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:05:18.748 --> 00:05:20.540
AUDIENCE: Are we supposed
to do both sides?

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:22.551
AMIT GANDHI: What?

00:05:22.551 --> 00:05:24.218
AUDIENCE: Like, are
we supposed to have,

00:05:24.218 --> 00:05:25.934
like, both sides connected?

00:05:25.934 --> 00:05:28.310
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:05:52.162 --> 00:05:53.662
AUDIENCE: Do I need
a load for that?

00:05:53.662 --> 00:05:56.022
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:56.970
AMIT GANDHI: You bet.

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:58.845
AUDIENCE: These are
really pretty in the sun.

00:05:58.845 --> 00:06:01.515
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:06:01.515 --> 00:06:03.640
AUDIENCE: So yeah, so when
you're checking voltage,

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:06.525
you need to sort of
do it in parallel.

00:06:06.525 --> 00:06:09.540
Like, check it across an
element to see how much voltage

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:11.850
is being dropped.

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:14.060
So in this circuit, the only--

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:15.328
so you have this battery.

00:06:15.328 --> 00:06:16.120
This is the source.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:18.322
And then the only load is this.

00:06:18.322 --> 00:06:19.780
And so this is
going to be dropping

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:25.140
all of the voltage that is being
collected by the solar panel.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:26.350
AUDIENCE: Getting anything?

00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:28.142
AUDIENCE: This is
getting a little current.

00:06:28.142 --> 00:06:30.430
AUDIENCE: You don't have
any current in this one.

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:31.030
I mean, the--

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:32.060
AUDIENCE: You're right.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:32.560
Sorry.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:41.830
2.36.

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:43.363
AUDIENCE: That's milliamps?

00:06:43.363 --> 00:06:44.030
AUDIENCE: Volts.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:44.863
AUDIENCE: Oh, volts.

00:06:44.863 --> 00:06:46.468
AUDIENCE: Yeah,
we're checking volts.

00:06:49.396 --> 00:06:50.930
AUDIENCE: Then you
don't need the--

00:06:50.930 --> 00:06:52.180
AUDIENCE: Then you're on this.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:56.230
AUDIENCE: Yeah, because this
becomes, like, the finishing.

00:06:56.230 --> 00:06:58.238
AUDIENCE: Oh, so in
that case, are we--

00:06:58.238 --> 00:06:58.780
I don't know.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:00.238
We're going to
still check voltage.

00:07:00.238 --> 00:07:03.600
AUDIENCE: Yeah, we'll check
the voltage across this one.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.120
AUDIENCE: Got 1.2--

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:06.120
2.4 for the whole thing.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:07.582
That's what it says.

00:07:07.582 --> 00:07:08.540
AMIT GANDHI: Excellent.

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:10.185
AUDIENCE: Nice work.

00:07:10.185 --> 00:07:10.810
AUDIENCE: 2.56.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:15.240
AUDIENCE: 0.58 across
the whole cell.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:16.973
AUDIENCE: And what
was the resistance?

00:07:16.973 --> 00:07:17.765
AUDIENCE: 100 ohms.

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:22.880
AUDIENCE: It should change it.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:24.374
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:07:24.374 --> 00:07:26.166
AUDIENCE: No, it's
still wired across four,

00:07:26.166 --> 00:07:27.610
and it covers one of them.

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:29.318
AUDIENCE: Can we just--
yeah, can we just

00:07:29.318 --> 00:07:31.770
go inside and do a data switch?

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:33.400
AUDIENCE: All those
three numbers.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:35.580
AUDIENCE: Yeah, yeah.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:36.420
AUDIENCE: I'm sorry.

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:37.080
AUDIENCE: I guess there's--

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:37.990
AUDIENCE: I don't think we--

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:38.790
AUDIENCE: Oh are
we going to do--

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:39.540
AUDIENCE: On the first
panel, we didn't cover up

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:40.207
a quarter of it.

00:07:40.207 --> 00:07:42.415
AUDIENCE: Are we going to
do a cover on one of these?

00:07:42.415 --> 00:07:43.499
AUDIENCE: Yeah, basically.

00:07:43.499 --> 00:07:44.550
AUDIENCE: Same thing?

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:46.710
AUDIENCE: Do you want to
just cover the quarter

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:47.740
panel for the first one?

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:48.865
We didn't actually do that.

00:07:48.865 --> 00:07:51.480
AUDIENCE: Sure, let's do that.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:53.860
We just covered one entire cell.

00:07:56.752 --> 00:07:59.650
[INAUDIBLE], right?

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:01.200
AUDIENCE: Sure.

